#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""mkrows16.py > rows : the round 16 ledger rows (eight cells each, no bare pipe inside a cell)."""
T = 'Docs/Decompiler/tools/a3_round16'
PC = 'SE_GENERATED_PRINTEDC=only SE_GENERATED_PRINTEDC_WORDS='
rows = []
def row(id_, sev, finding, fixture, status='open'):
    cells = [id_, '3', sev, finding, fixture, status, 'area 3 round 16 review', '']
    for c in cells:
        assert '|' not in c.replace('\\|', ''), (id_, c)
        assert '\n' not in c
    rows.append('| ' + ' | '.join(cells) + ' |')

row('A3-144', 'P1',
 "Regression of patch 0072 (A3-130's fix): a loop that stores through a pointer reached by two or more frame slots prints a text that reads the slots through a pointer variable nothing set. `slot0` (frame+32) holds &S (frame+4), `slot1` (frame+36) holds &slot0, `r3 = 3`; `L: mov.l @(36,r15),r1 ; mov.l @r1,r1 ; mov.l r5,@r1 ; add #4,r1 ; mov.l r1,@(32,r15) ; add r6,r5 ; dt r3 ; bf L`; then S+0 and S+4 are read directly and added. The code returns 0x6721 for r4..r7 = 11223344 55 6677 80000001. The text at 63e5b18 is `xStack_24` (slot0, the word that holds &S) declared and never assigned, `pxStack_20 = &xStack_24;`, `do { *(int4 *)*pxStack_20 = param_2; param_2 = param_2 + param_3; ... } while (iVar1 != 0); return iStack_40 + iStack_3c;`: the setup stores of both slots are dropped, the loop stores through whatever the unset local holds (the compiled text segfaults), and the return reads locals nothing wrote. Two, three and four slots alike (`LOOP L2` to `L4`), with the pointer stepped and written back to slot0 every trip (the base prints `piStack_24 = &iStack_40; ... *piStack_24 = param_2; piStack_24 = piStack_24 + 1;`, right) and with the pointer left alone (the base loses the loop's stores, `LEDGER BAD`: A3-130's shape inside a loop; the head is wrong in the other way). One slot is right on both builds. Not drawn by `frame124`'s chain stream or by `frame15.py` (no loop). In 1,800 call-free `frame16.py` fixtures (printed-C differential, seeds 11, 12 and 21 to 24) 28 loop fixtures with 2 to 4 slots and 3 index fixtures with 4 slots are findings at 63e5b18 and are none at 2598c9b (545 findings the other way: patch 0072's fixes). The frame ledger says `LEDGER ok` and the function is withheld ('a return not to its caller'), so nothing but the printed-C differential and a reading shows it; PseudoEval cannot evaluate a loop. Mechanism (by reading, not traced): 0072 keeps the guards of a STORE it calls pending until its pointer is settled (`reprocessPendingStores`); in a loop the pointer's own chain is read after the previous trip's STORE, so the guard INDIRECT on slot0/slot1 sits between the setup and the reload, the reload is no constant frame address, the LOAD through it is never folded into a stack read, and the pointer never settles. The stack writes only that LOAD would read are then dead code. The same two missing pieces as A3-145: an unfolded LOAD through a pointer that may be a frame address does not keep the frame's writes alive.",
 "`%s/mkloop.py` (`python3 mkloop.py 0 3 sum`: `LOOP L1` to `L4`), `mkloop2.py` (`nowb`, `wb`, `wbdirect`, `wb1`), `frame16.py` mode loop; LOOP L2 06009000 args 11223344 55 6677 80000001: `4F22 7FC0 61F3 7104 1F18 61F3 7120 1F19 E303 51F9 6112 2152 7104 1F18 356C 4310 8BF7 50F1 53F2 303C 7F40 4F26 000B 0009` (sts.l pr ; add #-64,r15 ; slot0=&S ; slot1=&slot0 ; r3=3 ; loop ; S+0 + S+4). Replay: `%s\"4F22 7FC0 61F3 7104 1F18 61F3 7120 1F19 E303 51F9 6112 2152 7104 1F18 356C 4310 8BF7 50F1 53F2 303C 7F40 4F26 000B 0009\"`: finding `crashed` (segmentation fault of the compiled text) at 63e5b18, no finding at 2598c9b (`LOOP L3`, `L4` the same: 3 of 4 `mkloop.py` fixtures). Wrong: the text above. Right: `piStack_24 = &iStack_40; do { *piStack_24 = param_2; piStack_24 = piStack_24 + 1; ...} while (...); return iStack_40 + iStack_3c;` (what 2598c9b prints for the write-back form) or the chain's stores printed. Oracle: the printed-C differential." % (T, PC))

row('A3-145', 'P1',
 "A non-constant-index STORE anywhere in the frame makes a read through a pointer reloaded from a frame slot read stack words nothing wrote: the stores that filled them are dropped. One slot, no loop, no call: `S` (frame+8) is filled by four direct word stores (r4 to r7), slot0 (frame+32) holds &S, `mov r15,r1 ; add #8,r1 ; mov r6,r0 ; and #12,r0 ; mov.l r5,@(r0,r1)` (an indexed store into S), then `mov.l @(32,r15),r1 ; mov.l @r1,r0 ; add #4,r1 ; mov.l @r1,r3 ; xor r3,r0`: the code returns 0x80000001 for r4 = 0, r5 = 0x80000001, r6 = 0xFF (S[0] xor S[1]); the text is `puStack_24 = auStack_3c; *(xunknown4 *)((int4)auStack_3c + (param_3 & 0xc)) = param_2; uVar1 = *puStack_24; return uVar1 ^ puStack_24[1];` with no store of the four fill words anywhere, the compiled text returns 0. Reading S directly after the indexed store (`mov.l @(8,r15),r0`) is right (the fills print as `auStack_3c[0] = param_1;`); a constant-offset store instead of the indexed one is right; the fill through the pointer or direct, an indexed store through the reloaded pointer or through `r15`, two to four slots: all wrong. Wrong on 2598c9b exactly the same for one slot (the head's two to four slot form is wrong another way: the chain's own stores are dropped as in A3-144, `puVar2 = (uint4 *)*pxStack_20;` with `xStack_24` never assigned; the base loses the indexed store's chain stores). The reload read of the array, a real -O0 shape: `int a[4]; int *p = a; ... a[i] = x; return *p ^ p[1];`. The indexed store need not touch S: one into another local (frame+44, `IDX3 other L1`, `mkidx3.py`) gives the same wrong text for one slot and a crash for two slots at both builds. 11 of the 1,800 call-free `frame16.py` fixtures (index mode, one slot) are this finding at both builds, plus the 3 index fixtures of A3-144 at four slots. The frame ledger says ok (the engine's records mark the fills 'a frame write gone unread' and the final read 'folded into ordinary memory', which `ReferenceOk` does not take for a loss) and `PseudoEval` says `ORACLE agrees` for the same text (it reads the unwritten array as the run's value); the printed-C differential finds it (`mismatch`, run 80000001, text 0). Mechanism (by reading, not traced): the indexed STORE keeps its guard on every stack range (it may write any of them), so the slot's reload is the output of a guard INDIRECT and not a constant; the LOAD through it is never folded into a stack read; the dead-code pass sees no read of the fill words. A pointer to a frame word that survives as a LOAD must keep every frame write it may read alive.",
 "`%s/mkidx3.py` (the indexed store into S or into another local, one or two slots), `%s/mkidx2.py` (matrix: store idxrel/idxdir/constrel/none, read rel/dir, fill dir/rel; wrong are `idxdir/rel` and `idxrel/rel` with either fill), `mkidx.py` (one to four slots), `frame16.py` mode index; IDX2 st=idxdir rd=rel fill=dir 06009000: `4F22 7FC0 61F3 7108 1F18 61F3 7108 2142 61F3 710C 2152 61F3 7110 2162 61F3 7114 2172 61F3 7108 6063 C90C 0156 51F8 6012 7104 6312 203A 7F40 4F26 000B 0009 0009`. Replay: `%s\"4F22 7FC0 61F3 7108 1F18 61F3 7108 2142 61F3 710C 2152 61F3 7110 2162 61F3 7114 2172 61F3 7108 6063 C90C 0156 51F8 6012 7104 6312 203A 7F40 4F26 000B 0009 0009\"`: finding `mismatch` (run=80000001, pseudo=00000000) at 63e5b18 and 2598c9b. Wrong: `return uVar1 ^ puStack_24[1];` over an array nothing wrote. Right: the fills printed (`auStack_3c[0] = param_1; ...`) as the direct-read form prints them. Oracle: printed-C differential only. The 4,725 corpus texts of each build have no instance (`uninit16.py`: 9 hits, each a pointer-walked array written by `*(T *)((int4)p + k) = v`)." % (T, T, PC))

row('A3-146', 'P1',
 "A frame address whose slot is partly overwritten by an input byte or halfword is stored through as if it were a data pointer (hand-written; the same on 2598c9b). `slot0 = &S` (frame+32); `mov r4,r0 ; mov r15,r2 ; add #35,r2 ; mov.b r0,@r2` overwrites the low byte of the slot (big endian: the byte at +3) with r4; `mov.l @(32,r15),r1 ; mov.l r7,@r1`; then S+0, S+4, S+8 and S+12 (filled before by r5, r6, r7, r5) are read and combined. The pointer is the frame address with its low byte replaced, which is S itself when r4 = 0xC4 (the frame is at 0x0600EFC4): the code returns 0x70 for r4 = 0xC4, r5..r7 = 0x10 0x20 0x30 and 0x80 for r4 = 0xC8; the text is `xStack_24 = (uint4 *)CONCAT31((unkint3)((uint4)&iStack_3c >> 8),param_1); ... *xStack_24 = param_4; return (iStack_3c + iStack_38 ^ uStack_34) + iStack_30;`, which returns 0x10 for both: the store through the pointer is not taken for a write of the frame, although its high 24 bits are the frame address's. The halfword form (`mov.w r0,@(34,r15)`) is the same for r4 = 0xEFC4. The pointer the STORE gets is a PIECE of a shifted piece of the frame address and the data byte (the text's `CONCAT31`); by reading, not traced, `FrameEscape` takes neither that PIECE nor the shift for a form of the frame address, so the STORE is a store through plain data. It is the A3-124 class through a form round 14's laundering fuzzers (`escfuzz14.py`: mask, swap, xtrct, MACH/MACL, the T bit, ...) do not draw: a partial overwrite of the pointer's slot in memory. The text does not compile either (`*xStack_24` of a scalar-typed local), so the printed-C differential reports `not-compilable`; the driver's run line is the oracle. P1 as a wrong printed value; hand-written, like A3-13: the accepting reason would be that no compiler emits it.",
 "`%s/mkbyte.py` (`BYTE byte`, `BYTE half`), 06009000, args `000000c4 10 20 30` (code 0x70, text 0x10) and `000000c8 10 20 30` (0x80, 0x10), `0000efc4 10 20 30` for the halfword form: `4F22 7FC0 61F3 7108 1F18 61F3 7108 2152 61F3 710C 2162 61F3 7110 2172 61F3 7114 2152 6043 62F3 7223 2200 51F8 2172 50F2 53F3 303C 53F4 203A 53F5 303C 7F40 4F26 000B 0009` (halfword: `... 6043 62F3 7222 2201 ...`). Wrong: the text above, `ORACLE REJECTS returns 00000010`. Right: the store through `xStack_24` counted as a write of the frame (guard every local) or the pointer's pieces taken as an unmodeled form. Oracle: the driver's run line." % T)

row('A3-147', 'P2',
 "Oracle gap behind A3-144 and A3-145: none of the frame instruments sees them, and the one that does is limited to call-free functions. In the 2,340 `frame16.py` fixtures run through the driver (frame ledger, `PseudoEval`) the ledger said `BAD` for 343, every one a function that publishes a frame address in a global (A3-141's class, `g=1`), and for none of the 1,997 others, including all A3-144 and A3-145 fixtures (`LEDGER ok`: the engine's records say the fills 'went unread', which `ReferenceOk` takes for a dead store when the function still has a printed read of the frame through a pointer; a printed `*p` where `p` holds `&local` is a read of the local that the ledger does not count). `PseudoEval` says `ORACLE agrees` for A3-145's text (the same fixture the printed-C differential rejects), says `cannot evaluate the pseudocode` for 60 to 70% of the fixtures (a loop, an array, a pointer to a local) and `REJECTS` falsely for a signed piece (`(int4)xStack_3a` of an `xunknown2`: the 9 fixtures it rejected in two runs, 7 of them lent, all agree in the printed-C differential). The printed-C differential is the only value oracle for these shapes, sees call-free functions only, and cannot compile a function that publishes a frame address in a global (`pxRam0600f000` undeclared: 7% of the `g=1` fixtures, A3-148). The generator's own streams (`GenFrame124Fn`, `GenFrame130Fn` from A3-134) draw no loop, no indexed store and no partly overwritten pointer, so the nightly range cannot reach A3-144 to A3-146 at all. A text-level check was tried (`uninit16.py`: a local nothing assigns whose address is kept in a pointer that the text dereferences): it flags A3-144's text and has 54 hits in the 4,725 corpus texts of each build, the same set at 2598c9b, all read as false (a callee's output, a store through the pointer, a pointer handed to a call); it is no oracle.",
 "`%s/f16run.sh`, `f16sum.py` (LEDGER, ORACLE, preserved-mask check), `f16pc.py` (printed-C differential), `frame16.py`, `uninit16.py`; `F16_PC=1 F16_NOCALL=1 python3 frame16.py 11 300` through `f16pc.py` on both builds (`cmp16.sh`). Right: the generator's chain stream draws loops (a loop-carried pointer, stepped and written back), indexed stores through the reloaded pointer and a partly overwritten pointer slot, and the frame ledger counts a printed access through a pointer to a local as a read or write of it." % T)

row('A3-148', 'P3',
 "Oracle gap: the printed-C differential cannot compile a function that publishes a frame address in a global (`pxRam0600f000 = axStack_44;`: the name is declared nowhere; `not-compilable` for 53 call-free `g=1` fixtures of `frame16.py` (seeds 11, 12 and 21 to 24) at both builds and for 23 more at 63e5b18 only, where 2598c9b had printed the whole chain as locals and compiled, and 63e5b18 folds it to `return 0xc5;`, right by the run), and A3-141 leaves `LEDGER BAD` constant for the class. So a function of this class has neither a value oracle nor a usable ledger: a lost store in one would be invisible. A hand check of the callee-reads-the-global shape (`GCALL`: `&S` published, a pointer read back, two stores through it, a lent callee that reads `*G` and `G[1]`) is right on both builds (`xStack_3c = param_3; xStack_38 = param_2;` printed before the call). The same fixtures show the loop shape of A3-137: a pointer walked past the end of its array symbol into the next local (`acStack_38 [8]`, a byte store 9 bytes on, read as `iStack_30`) is printed as the array walk and mismatches the compiled text's layout (value right by hand).",
 "`%s/mkgcall.py` (GCALL chain/glob/globdirect), `frame16.py` mode global; `F16_PC=1 F16_NOCALL=1 python3 frame16.py 21 300` through `f16pc.py`: `F16.21.41 global L2 N7` (`not-compilable`, `return 0xc5;` right by the run). Right: the harness declares the `*Ram<addr>` names the text uses (`static xunknown4 *pxRam0600f000;`) so the differential compares them, and A3-141's records are fixed." % T)

print('\n'.join(rows))
